N,R,S="N","R","S"
SCENES={
"title":[(N,"Operation Gate. The Learning Machine. Machine learning for GATE Data Science and A I, twenty twenty seven."),
(N,"Seven rooms, seven twists. Every idea is run in plain Python with comments, so you can see the logic and change it.")],
"reg":[(N,"Room one. Regression."),
(R,"What does it mean for a machine to learn a line?"),
(S,"Think of hours studied against marks. The points are scattered, and we want the one line that is closest to all of them. Closest means the smallest sum of squared errors."),
(S,"For one variable there is a formula. The slope is the covariance of x and y, divided by the variance of x. The line must pass through the mean point. The code on the left does exactly that."),
(R,"How good is it?"),
(S,"R squared. One minus the unexplained spread over the total spread. Here it prints point nine nine seven, so the line explains almost everything. The slope is one point nine nine, the bias is point oh five."),
(S,"For bigger problems there is no neat formula, so we learn by gradient descent. Guess a line, measure the error, nudge the slope and the bias against the error slope, and repeat three thousand times."),
(S,"To stop a model from memorising, add a penalty on large weights. Ridge penalises squared weights and shrinks them. Lasso penalises absolute weights, and can push some exactly to zero."),
(N,"Twist one. Adding more features can only raise R squared on the training data, even if the features are random noise. Always judge a model on data it has not seen.")],
"cls":[(N,"Room two. Classification."),
(R,"How do we turn a line into a yes or no?"),
(S,"Logistic regression squeezes the line's score through the sigmoid, one over one plus e to the minus z. Score zero gives one half. Score two gives point eight eight one. The output is a probability."),
(S,"K nearest neighbours asks the closest k training points to vote. Naive Bayes multiplies the evidence of each feature, pretending they are independent."),
(S,"Now measure it. Of a thousand cases, forty are caught, ten are false alarms, twenty are missed, and nine hundred thirty are correctly rejected. Accuracy is point nine seven."),
(R,"That sounds great."),
(S,"Look deeper. Precision is point eight, recall is point six six seven, and F one is point seven two seven. Precision asks, when I say yes, am I right. Recall asks, of the real yes cases, how many did I find."),
(N,"Twist two. A useless model that always says no already scores point nine four accuracy here, because the positives are rare. On imbalanced data, accuracy lies. Use precision, recall and F one.")],
"tree":[(N,"Room three. Decision trees."),
(R,"How does a tree decide which question to ask first?"),
(S,"It asks the question that makes the answers purest. Purity is measured by entropy. Entropy is zero when all labels agree, and highest when they are evenly mixed."),
(S,"Take fourteen days, nine yes and five no. The entropy is point nine four zero. Now split by outlook. Sunny has two yes, three no. Overcast has four yes. Rain has three yes, two no."),
(S,"The weighted entropy after the split is point six nine four, so the information gain is point two four seven. The tree tries every feature and picks the largest gain."),
(R,"Is entropy the only measure?"),
(S,"No. Gini impurity is one minus the sum of squared proportions, here point four five nine. Trees that grow too deep memorise, so we prune them. A random forest averages many trees, each on a random sample."),
(N,"Twist three. Information gain loves features with many values, like an I D column, because each value is perfectly pure. Gain ratio fixes that bias.")],
"svm":[(N,"Room four. Support vector machines."),
(R,"There are infinitely many separating lines. Which one does an S V M choose?"),
(S,"The one with the widest empty street between the classes. That street is the margin, and its width is two over the length of w. Maximising margin means minimising the length of w."),
(S,"The code uses four points and the line half x plus half y minus one. The two inner points score exactly one and minus one. They sit on the edge of the street. The margin width is two point eight two eight."),
(R,"What are the other points doing?"),
(S,"Nothing. Their hinge loss is zero, because they are safely outside the street. Only the points on the edge, the support vectors, hold the line in place."),
(S,"If the classes are not separable by a line, add a new feature. The kernel trick adds x squared. Points one dimensional, minus three and three on the outside, become separable by the rule x squared bigger than four."),
(N,"Twist four. Delete every point that is not a support vector and retrain. The line does not move at all. Only the edge points matter.")],
"nn":[(N,"Room five. Neural networks."),
(R,"What is a neuron, really?"),
(S,"A weighted sum plus a bias, passed through an activation like sigmoid or ReLU. A network stacks layers of them. The activation is vital. Without it, many layers collapse into one line."),
(S,"A single neuron draws one straight line. The X O R problem, output one only when the inputs differ, cannot be split by one line. That famous failure stalled the field for years."),
(S,"A hidden layer fixes it. Each hidden unit draws its own line, and the output combines them."),
(S,"The code builds a tiny network with four hidden units and trains it on X O R. It runs the input forward to get a prediction, then pushes the error backward to update every weight. That is backpropagation."),
(R,"What does it print?"),
(S,"Zero, one, one, zero. The network learned X O R."),
(S,"Backpropagation is the chain rule. The output error times the slope of the sigmoid tells each weight how guilty it is. Gradient descent then moves every weight a little against its guilt."),
(N,"Twist five. A deep network with only linear activations is still a single line. The bend that makes learning powerful comes entirely from the activation.")],
"ev":[(N,"Room six. Model evaluation."),
(R,"Why is training error not enough?"),
(S,"Because a model can memorise. Simple models have high bias, they miss the pattern. Complex models have high variance, they chase the noise. Total error is bias squared plus variance plus irreducible noise."),
(S,"As complexity grows, training error keeps falling, but validation error falls and then rises. That U shape tells you where to stop."),
(S,"Look at the code. K nearest neighbours with k equal one memorises, so the training error is zero. But leave one out cross validation hides each point in turn and predicts it. The error is four point nine two."),
(R,"And the middle k?"),
(S,"With k equal three, the training error is higher, one point eight, but the validation error is the lowest, three point one six. K equal five smooths too much and the validation error rises again."),
(S,"K fold cross validation splits the data into k parts, trains on k minus one, tests on the rest, and averages. Every point is tested exactly once."),
(N,"Twist six. The best model is not the one with the smallest training error. It is the one with the smallest cross validation error.")],
"un":[(N,"Room seven. Clustering and P C A."),
(R,"There are no labels. What does the machine learn?"),
(S,"Structure. K means picks k centres. Every point joins its nearest centre. Each centre moves to the mean of its points. Repeat until nothing changes."),
(S,"The code starts at one, one and five, seven. After a few rounds the centres settle at one point two five, one point five, and three point nine, five point one, with two and five points."),
(R,"And P C A?"),
(S,"P C A rotates the axes to the directions of greatest variance. Those directions are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix, and the eigenvalues are the variances."),
(S,"The code finds eigenvalues one point two eight four and point oh four nine. The first component keeps ninety six point three percent of the variance. Ten points in two dimensions are almost a line."),
(N,"Twist seven. K means depends on its starting centres, and a bad start gives a bad answer, so we run it several times. And P C A is exactly S V D of the centred data.")],
"dsa":[(N,"Lab. The complexity of learning."),
(R,"Which of these algorithms is expensive?"),
(S,"Count the work. K nearest neighbours has no training, but every query measures the distance to all n points. With ten thousand points, that is ten thousand distances, for each query."),
(S,"Choosing the best three can use a heap, which costs n log k, instead of sorting everything. The code confirms the heap gives the same three neighbours."),
(N,"K means costs n times k times d per round. For ten thousand points, three clusters and two dimensions, that is sixty thousand operations. A decision tree split sorts a feature, which is n log n."),
(R,"So which one scales best?"),
(S,"Gradient descent costs n times d per epoch, linear in the data. That is why it trains huge models. Matching the algorithm to the data size is the D S A skill."),
(N,"D S A twist. A heap of size k turns an n log n sort into n log k. For small k, that is almost linear.")],
"wk":[(N,"Walkthrough. Two learning loops, line by line."),
(S,"Gradient descent first. One training example, x equals one, truth y equals one. The weight starts at point five, and the learning rate is point five."),
(S,"Step one. The forward pass, z equals w times x, point five. The error is z minus y, minus point five."),
(S,"The slope of the loss with respect to w is the error times x, minus point five. We move against it. The new weight is point five minus point five times minus point five, which is point seven five."),
(S,"Step two. Now z is point seven five, the error is minus point two five, and the weight becomes point eight seven five. Step three, point nine three eight."),
(N,"Main logic. Predict, measure the error, move the weight against the slope. Each step closes half the gap. The weight is heading to one."),
(S,"Now one round of k means. The data is one, two, nine, ten. The centroids start at one and ten."),
(S,"Assign step. For each point, measure the distance to both centroids, and add it to the closer group. One and two go to the first group. Nine and ten go to the second."),
(S,"Update step. Each centroid becomes the mean of its group. One and two average to one point five. Nine and ten average to nine point five."),
(N,"Main logic. Assign, then update. Repeat until nothing moves. Each round can only make the clusters tighter.")],
"end":[(N,"The machine has learned. Regression, classification, trees, S V Ms, neural networks, evaluation, clustering and P C A."),
(R,"So learning is guess, measure the error, and nudge."),
(S,"One film left, artificial intelligence. See you there.")],
}
